WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.280
Tuesday's job is finished.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:09.340
By Friday, the office is still rebuilding what the crew already completed
from messages, photos, and paper.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:19.100
Without TCS, field photos, quantities, and closeout details scatter across channels,
leaving the job record incomplete and invisible.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.660
The customer waits for status.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.080
The invoice waits for a tally.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.660
The payment clock keeps waiting on the office team.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:38.860
Betty retypes the same work again by hand,
while paperwork rises above the job folder and collapses across her crowded Friday desk.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:49.200
With TCS, Freddie captures the map, codes, quantities, and photos once in the field,
and one job record begins moving.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:58.280
Job tracking becomes the hub, sending one approved record to crews,
costing, maps, and billing without retyping.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.160
Now Pete sees the current status.
Betty reviews instead of reconstructing,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.620
and the invoice starts from what the crew submitted.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:19.680
One field record now carries the whole job downstream,
turning Tuesday's completed work into visibility, cost, billing, and financial control.
